 method-name catalogues. Dropping it turns four linters into noise\ngenerators. The docstring on iter_axis_set_assignments says so.\n\nTHE SHAPE OF THE PROBLEM. The filter conflates two questions: \"is this\nconstant ABOUT the axis?\" (what we want) and \"is the axis's name spelled in\nthis identifier?\" (what is tested). A consumer named for what it MAPS TO\nrather than what it maps FROM -- AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP is named for the taint\nlabel, DEFERRED_CROSSING_SHADOWS for the shadowing relation -- is about the\naxis while never naming it.\n\nOPTIONS, none costed:\n  (a) An explicit per-axis INCLUDED_TARGET_NAMES allowlist beside the existing\n      excluded_target_names. Cheap, exact, and it does not weaken the filter;\n      the cost is that it only ever contains what someone remembered to add,\n      which is the same class of gap as the one being fixed.\n  (b) A second pass keyed on VALUE membership rather than name -- collect every\n      module-level string collection and report only those whose values are a\n      non-trivial SUBSET of the registry. Self-maintaining, and the \"non-trivial\"\n      threshold is where the false positives live; needs measurement before it\n      is worth anything.\n  (c) Rename the two constants to contain the axis substring. Cheapest to\n      implement, worst as a rule: it makes a linter's reach depend on\n      identifier fashion, and the next consumer will be named badly again.\n\nMEASURE FIRST, and the measurement is cheap: run option (b) in report-only\nmode over all four axes at several subset thresholds and count how many\nnon-registry constants it would drag in. If (b) is quiet, it dominates (a);\nif it is noisy, (a) is the honest answer and the residual gap gets written\ndown rather than closed.\n\nNOT KNOWN: whether the two named constants have actually DRIFTED today. They\nhave never been checked, which is the point -- their correctness is currently\nan accident rather than a gate. Check them as step zero; a drift found there\nis a separate bug and should be filed as one."  # bf30
